703
00:41:06,768 --> 00:41:08,378
will come the looters.

704
00:41:08,466 --> 00:41:09,989
And then the troublemakers.

705
00:41:11,947 --> 00:41:14,080
And I will be unable
to protect you.

706
00:41:15,821 --> 00:41:17,083
Unless?

707
00:41:17,170 --> 00:41:19,302
Unless I am your husband.

708
00:41:20,869 --> 00:41:22,480
Unless I am king.

709
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:29,965
I would hate to see anything
unfortunate befall you...

710
00:41:31,097 --> 00:41:34,666
Or your son in such

711
00:41:34,753 --> 00:41:36,929
rudderless times as these.

712
00:41:39,279 --> 00:41:42,108
It is time to make
your choice, Penelope.

713
00:41:45,677 --> 00:41:47,896
Before someone makes it for you.

714
00:42:12,312 --> 00:42:15,445
Alkimos, get some beeswax.

715
00:42:15,533 --> 00:42:18,100
Focus repairs on
the prow of the ship.

716
00:42:18,187 --> 00:42:20,712
That storm really took
a bite out of our hide.

717
00:42:20,799 --> 00:42:23,149
But we need the ship
to have her speed back.

718
00:42:23,236 --> 00:42:24,237
Yes, my king.

719
00:42:26,761 --> 00:42:30,983
The sea herself added another
day and a half to our journey

720
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:33,899
that we do not have to spare.

721
00:42:33,986 --> 00:42:36,118
The woes of my
heart upon this sea.

722
00:42:38,599 --> 00:42:40,688
What storm do you
think was worse?

723
00:42:40,775 --> 00:42:42,690
The one we just sailed through,
or the one that is on your brow?

724
00:42:44,779 --> 00:42:46,520
The storm to come.

725
00:42:46,607 --> 00:42:48,174
The journey ahead.

726
00:42:48,261 --> 00:42:49,131
The journey home.

727
00:42:50,524 --> 00:42:52,352
Yes.

728
00:42:52,439 --> 00:42:55,311
Polites, is I must get home
no matter what the cost.

729
00:42:55,398 --> 00:42:56,791
I must.

730
00:42:56,878 --> 00:42:58,924
I wonder if the costs
already paid.

731
00:42:59,011 --> 00:43:00,534
No, Polites...

732
00:43:00,621 --> 00:43:03,102
The costs were paid
both here and back in Troy.

733
00:43:04,494 --> 00:43:06,801
With flesh and blood.

734
00:43:06,888 --> 00:43:08,194
And this.
This is our reward, is it?

735
00:43:08,281 --> 00:43:10,544
More hardship.

736
00:43:10,631 --> 00:43:12,502
More storms.
Rough seas.

737
00:43:12,590 --> 00:43:14,896
I cannot control the fates.
Nor the seas.

738
00:43:14,983 --> 00:43:16,158
You forget that
I’ve seen you

739
00:43:16,245 --> 00:43:17,899
perform miracles
before, Odysseus.

740
00:43:17,986 --> 00:43:19,945
Miracles even
the gods couldn’t choose.

741
00:43:20,032 --> 00:43:22,034
If this was my kingdom, yes.

742
00:43:22,121 --> 00:43:23,905
But this is not my kingdom.

743
00:43:23,992 --> 00:43:25,690
Back home in my kingdom--

744
00:43:25,777 --> 00:43:26,734
By the gods of the sea!

745
00:43:32,044 --> 00:43:32,958
The sea has teeth!

746
00:43:40,139 --> 00:43:41,183
By the gods!

747
00:43:41,270 --> 00:43:43,272
Charybdis!

748
00:43:43,359 --> 00:43:46,145
It’s another one of Poseidon’s
weapons of vengeance.

749
00:43:46,232 --> 00:43:48,190
You didn’t stop Poseidon.

750
00:43:48,277 --> 00:43:49,583
You just made him more angry.

751
00:43:54,327 --> 00:43:56,982
Alkimos, come steer.

752
00:43:57,069 --> 00:43:58,723
Steer us around
the outside of it.

753
00:43:58,810 --> 00:44:00,899
My king, the repairs
aren’t finished yet.

754
00:44:00,986 --> 00:44:02,814
Without them, we won’t
be able to make that turn.

755
00:44:02,901 --> 00:44:04,076
It will be almost impossible.

756
00:44:04,163 --> 00:44:06,600
I will make that turn.
I will.

757
00:44:22,094 --> 00:44:23,356
The sea!

758
00:44:23,443 --> 00:44:24,574
It has swallowed so many,

759
00:44:24,662 --> 00:44:26,054
but I had no idea
it had a mouth!

760
00:44:28,100 --> 00:44:30,102
My king, the winds!

761
00:44:30,189 --> 00:44:31,059
We’re losing speed!

762
00:44:33,409 --> 00:44:35,107
Odysseus, what do we do?

763
00:44:35,194 --> 00:44:38,414
We will not let it
dissuade us off our course.

764
00:44:38,501 --> 00:44:39,981
Push, Alkimos!

765
00:44:40,068 --> 00:44:41,069
Push, Polites!

766
00:44:41,156 --> 00:44:42,854
Push!

767
00:44:42,941 --> 00:44:46,422
We will ride the wind
and the waves to freedom!

768
00:44:46,509 --> 00:44:48,729
Aye, my king!

769
00:44:48,816 --> 00:44:49,687
Odysseus!

770
00:44:51,776 --> 00:44:52,690
Look!

771
00:44:55,997 --> 00:44:57,956
By the gods!
It follows!

772
00:44:59,914 --> 00:45:02,438
Use the gift, son of Laertes!
The gift!

773
00:45:02,525 --> 00:45:03,962
The gift the goddess
gave to you!

774
00:45:04,049 --> 00:45:05,615
If it can help us, use it!

775
00:45:05,703 --> 00:45:06,834
Soon, loyal Polites!

776
00:45:06,921 --> 00:45:08,662
Use it now!

777
00:45:08,749 --> 00:45:11,883
I will not let
Poseidon’s pet beat us.

778
00:45:11,970 --> 00:45:17,105
He will not have the glory of
knowing I took the easy way out.

779
00:45:17,192 --> 00:45:18,585
Move!

780
00:45:18,672 --> 00:45:19,673
He is right.

781
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:21,414
You should use it.

782
00:45:21,501 --> 00:45:24,852
It is too early in the game
for you to die, Odysseus.

783
00:45:24,939 --> 00:45:27,159
Your pride will be your ruin.

784
00:45:28,943 --> 00:45:33,165
You cannot win
this without help.

785
00:45:33,252 --> 00:45:36,081
I can beat this beast on my own.

786
00:45:36,168 --> 00:45:38,518
I do not need
your help, goddess.

787
00:45:38,605 --> 00:45:40,520
I will get home in
spite of you...

788
00:45:40,607 --> 00:45:42,391
and all the gods!

789
00:45:42,478 --> 00:45:45,525
I will waste no
more breath on you.

790
00:45:45,612 --> 00:45:49,137
Not when it can be used
for another purpose.

791
00:45:49,224 --> 00:45:50,835
My king...

792
00:45:50,922 --> 00:45:52,793
Who the hell is it
you’re talking to back there?

793
00:45:52,880 --> 00:45:55,013
I will beat this beast
without your help, goddess.

794
00:46:00,627 --> 00:46:02,629
The whirlpool!

795
00:46:02,716 --> 00:46:04,762
It’s coming!

796
00:46:04,849 --> 00:46:06,067
Better hold on to something.

797
00:46:08,374 --> 00:46:09,462
Hold on, men.

798
00:46:12,030 --> 00:46:14,293
It’s not just any whirlpool.

799
00:46:14,380 --> 00:46:16,382
It’s a creature of Poseidon.

800
00:46:16,469 --> 00:46:19,907
And if it’s hungry,
we must feed it.

801
00:46:19,994 --> 00:46:23,868
We must feed it
until its belly is full.

802
00:46:23,955 --> 00:46:24,999
Until it chokes!

803
00:46:25,086 --> 00:46:26,522
Come on!

804
00:46:26,609 --> 00:46:28,873
What do you mean feed it?
Feed it with what?

805
00:46:28,960 --> 00:46:32,441
Quick!
Or we’ve already lost.

806
00:46:38,099 --> 00:46:41,059
Yes, what the storm
has taken from us.

807
00:46:41,146 --> 00:46:42,625
Grab the ropes.

808
00:46:42,712 --> 00:46:44,453
Anything, planks, barrels.

809
00:46:44,540 --> 00:46:46,455
Grab anything you can.

810
00:46:46,542 --> 00:46:48,457
Absolute madness.
You went mad, old man.

811
00:46:53,071 --> 00:46:56,248
Feed this beast.
Fill its belly.

812
00:46:56,335 --> 00:46:58,119
And if not...

813
00:46:58,206 --> 00:47:00,774
I will choke it myself
with my own body.

814
00:47:04,560 --> 00:47:06,867
Looks like the sea must eat.

815
00:47:06,954 --> 00:47:08,651
The fishing traps.

816
00:47:08,738 --> 00:47:10,566
Yes, the provisions. Now!

817
00:47:10,653 --> 00:47:12,786
The fishing traps?
What are we going to eat?

818
00:47:12,873 --> 00:47:14,832
We don’t need them.

819
00:47:14,919 --> 00:47:16,877
We’ll be home in two days.

820
00:47:16,964 --> 00:47:19,880
Or we’ll be dead
trying to protect it.

821
00:47:19,967 --> 00:47:21,273
Choke on it!

822
00:47:21,360 --> 00:47:22,578
Choke on all of it!

823
00:47:26,017 --> 00:47:27,975
It’s working!
It’s working!

824
00:47:30,151 --> 00:47:32,414
Now what we need is distance.
Move!

825
00:47:37,332 --> 00:47:38,943
Hear me, gods!

826
00:47:39,030 --> 00:47:42,381
I am strong enough
to fight my own battles.

827
00:47:42,468 --> 00:47:44,862
Damn you! Damn you!

828
00:47:56,786 --> 00:47:58,876
Gods.

829
00:47:58,963 --> 00:48:01,095
Please, please help us.

830
00:48:02,662 --> 00:48:05,273
Father...

831
00:48:05,360 --> 00:48:07,449
Father, why have
you abandoned us?

832
00:48:12,802 --> 00:48:14,630
Odysseus is alive.

833
00:48:14,717 --> 00:48:16,937
Who... who are you?

834
00:48:17,024 --> 00:48:19,592
Save your breath, child.

835
00:48:19,679 --> 00:48:20,941
Do not fear me.

836
00:48:22,856 --> 00:48:26,425
How have you appeared like that?

837
00:48:26,512 --> 00:48:28,122
Is this some magician’s trick?

838
00:48:28,209 --> 00:48:29,863
A phantasm?

839
00:48:29,950 --> 00:48:32,431
Please do not vex me, spirit!

840
00:48:32,518 --> 00:48:35,086
Athena hears your prayers.

841
00:48:35,173 --> 00:48:36,957
And you must hear my words.

842
00:48:39,046 --> 00:48:42,615
Your father has
not abandoned you.

843
00:48:42,702 --> 00:48:45,183
And neither have the gods.

844
00:48:45,270 --> 00:48:46,967
I am doing all that I can.

845
00:48:49,230 --> 00:48:52,233
Never before have I
seen a man fight for his home

846
00:48:52,320 --> 00:48:56,585
so boldly as
your father does in war.

847
00:48:56,672 --> 00:48:58,457
He honors me.

848
00:48:58,544 --> 00:49:02,417
And for that, I am
doing all that I can.

849
00:49:04,071 --> 00:49:08,989
But now, my child,
I must ask the same of you.

850
00:49:09,076 --> 00:49:11,861
You must protect
your father’s house

851
00:49:11,949 --> 00:49:16,388
at all costs, from
within and from without.

852
00:49:16,475 --> 00:49:21,610
You must be prepared
to fight in war, yes.

853
00:49:21,697 --> 00:49:26,311
But you must also be careful,
watchful, and cunning.

854
00:49:28,530 --> 00:49:30,968
It is time for boys
to become men.

855
00:49:40,890 --> 00:49:42,980
I see Odysseus in you.

856
00:49:45,156 --> 00:49:46,853
Perhaps it will be enough.

857
00:49:50,900 --> 00:49:52,728
Yes.

858
00:49:52,815 --> 00:49:55,427
Perhaps it will be enough.

859
00:49:55,514 --> 00:49:58,517
Be vigilant, Telemachus.

860
00:49:58,604 --> 00:50:01,172
You will know it when
the moment comes.

861
00:50:04,479 --> 00:50:07,439
And then you
will strike...

862
00:50:07,526 --> 00:50:12,357
for your mother,
for your father, and for me.

863
00:50:17,014 --> 00:50:19,103
A toast, then.

864
00:50:19,190 --> 00:50:21,627
A toast to our health,

865
00:50:21,714 --> 00:50:27,546
and in gratitude to
my most gracious hosts.

866
00:50:27,633 --> 00:50:29,722
Who, if I might
speak freely,

867
00:50:29,809 --> 00:50:34,335
deserve a king who would
protect them at home,

868
00:50:34,422 --> 00:50:36,598
and not seek glory elsewhere.

869
00:50:39,079 --> 00:50:41,734
And I most certainly
could be that king.

870
00:50:44,650 --> 00:50:48,784
A kingdom so fine
deserves a ruler

871
00:50:48,871 --> 00:50:51,396
who would never
think to abandon it.

872
00:50:51,483 --> 00:50:54,181
This is the king’s house.

873
00:50:54,268 --> 00:50:55,530
Odysseus’s house.

874
00:50:55,617 --> 00:50:56,705
My father’s house.

875
00:50:57,967 --> 00:50:59,360
Ah.

876
00:50:59,447 --> 00:51:03,669
And where exactly is
your father, Telemachus?

877
00:51:03,756 --> 00:51:05,410
I don’t see him.

878
00:51:05,497 --> 00:51:09,414
All I see instead
is a home deserted,

879
00:51:09,501 --> 00:51:14,158
a kingdom undefended,
vulnerable,

880
00:51:14,245 --> 00:51:17,683
and of the king
himself some stories.

881
00:51:17,770 --> 00:51:20,338
The poets are talking
out on the street.

882
00:51:20,425 --> 00:51:24,385
Some say he’s lost at sea.

883
00:51:24,472 --> 00:51:29,129
Others, that he dallies
with Cersei herself.

884
00:51:29,216 --> 00:51:32,828
And I myself have heard
nothing but stories of

885
00:51:32,915 --> 00:51:36,049
monsters and treasure

886
00:51:36,136 --> 00:51:39,139
and violence
and such bloodshed.

887
00:51:42,664 --> 00:51:46,929
Whatever stories you’ve heard,
you should know this:

888
00:51:47,016 --> 00:51:52,152
while my father is gone
and in his absence,

889
00:51:52,239 --> 00:51:56,287
I am bound to
defend what is his.

890
00:52:01,248 --> 00:52:04,425
Well, if it’s a fight
you’re looking for, boy.

891
00:52:04,512 --> 00:52:07,646
Honorable Antinous.

892
00:52:07,733 --> 00:52:11,128
Strong words do harm
to the digestion.

893
00:52:11,215 --> 00:52:14,827
And violence is an ill
guest in this house or any.

894
00:52:20,049 --> 00:52:22,313
Please.

895
00:52:22,400 --> 00:52:23,792
Of course, my lady.

896
00:52:25,794 --> 00:52:27,405
Of course.

897
00:52:27,492 --> 00:52:33,237
But I wouldn’t need to
be a guest if my queen

898
00:52:33,324 --> 00:52:35,239
would take this suitor
to be her husband.

899
00:52:35,326 --> 00:52:37,371
I fear I cannot remarry.

900
00:52:40,853 --> 00:52:42,507
What?

901
00:52:42,594 --> 00:52:45,205
I cannot remarry
until I finish this cloak

902
00:52:45,292 --> 00:52:48,817
I’ve been
making for my son.

903
00:52:48,904 --> 00:52:51,864
It is only custom and proper.

904
00:52:51,951 --> 00:52:55,302
A parting gift for my
only flesh and blood.

905
00:52:57,565 --> 00:53:03,092
Before my duties as queen
to my new king

906
00:53:03,180 --> 00:53:07,184
fill my days and my hand.

907
00:53:10,317 --> 00:53:12,232
Indeed.

908
00:53:12,319 --> 00:53:16,410
Now far be it from me
to rush propriety.

909
00:53:16,497 --> 00:53:21,067
And I surely look forward
to viewing the finished product.

910
00:53:41,392 --> 00:53:46,571
Must the entire sea move
against Odysseus, Lord Poseidon?

911
00:53:46,658 --> 00:53:49,965
Behavior from such a god
seems small and petty.

912
00:53:50,052 --> 00:53:51,271
This isn’t petty.

913
00:53:51,358 --> 00:53:53,360
This is revenge!

914
00:53:53,447 --> 00:53:57,146
For my son, for what
Odysseus did to my son.

915
00:53:57,234 --> 00:54:03,152
Your son, Lord Poseidon, was
harming and murdering innocents.

916
00:54:03,240 --> 00:54:06,895
Perhaps he will
think twice next time.

917
00:54:06,982 --> 00:54:09,811
Enjoy your pet hero
while you can, Athena.

918
00:54:09,898 --> 00:54:11,073
With your gifts.

919
00:54:11,160 --> 00:54:12,553
Your meddling matters not.

920
00:54:12,640 --> 00:54:16,165
Odysseus will not
leave my ocean alive.

921
00:54:27,046 --> 00:54:29,091
Polites, how is she?

922
00:54:29,178 --> 00:54:30,789
Is she patched up yet?

923
00:54:30,876 --> 00:54:32,660
She’s looking pretty good.

924
00:54:32,747 --> 00:54:35,141
Pretty good,
a little bit more...

925
00:54:35,228 --> 00:54:36,751
beeswax and
she’ll be good to go.

926
00:54:36,838 --> 00:54:39,058
Good, good.

927
00:54:39,145 --> 00:54:41,539
Set a course for home, Alkimos.

928
00:54:41,626 --> 00:54:43,149
Yes, my king.

929
00:54:43,236 --> 00:54:46,848
For home, and for our rewards.

930
00:54:46,935 --> 00:54:49,242
Gold.

931
00:54:49,329 --> 00:54:53,420
Yes, yes, indeed, Polites.
Indeed.

932
00:54:53,507 --> 00:54:55,727
And just on that,
by the way, I’ve been meaning

933
00:54:55,814 --> 00:54:56,945
to tell you, meaning
to ask about...

934
00:54:57,032 --> 00:54:58,077
Odysseus!

935
00:55:01,820 --> 00:55:03,212
A monster!

936
00:55:03,300 --> 00:55:05,302
The Scylla!
Poseidon’s lap dog!

937
00:55:06,955 --> 00:55:08,217
Alkimos, no!

938
00:55:12,396 --> 00:55:13,310
Odysseus!

939
00:55:19,794 --> 00:55:22,231
What do we do?

940
00:55:22,319 --> 00:55:23,842
We try to outrun it.

941
00:55:23,929 --> 00:55:25,104
The wind...

942
00:55:25,191 --> 00:55:26,497
The wind will guide us.

943
00:55:31,415 --> 00:55:33,852
Yes... Yes, faster!

944
00:55:33,939 --> 00:55:35,070
Faster!

945
00:55:42,600 --> 00:55:43,731
No!

946
00:55:47,866 --> 00:55:50,521
No! There’s not enough wind
at our back!

947
00:55:51,783 --> 00:55:54,786
Not enough wind to fight!

948
00:55:54,873 --> 00:55:56,440
Only the monster at our heels!

949
00:55:56,527 --> 00:55:57,832
Damn my pride!

950
00:56:02,881 --> 00:56:05,623
Athena! Athena!

951
00:56:07,059 --> 00:56:08,277
I’m asking for help!

952
00:56:13,848 --> 00:56:15,328
Another gift, then?

953
00:56:15,415 --> 00:56:17,156
Goddess!
We need weapons!

954
00:56:19,288 --> 00:56:21,508
Ask, and it will be yours.

955
00:56:21,595 --> 00:56:24,816
Strategy. And sight.

956
00:56:24,903 --> 00:56:26,252
Sight.

957
00:56:26,339 --> 00:56:27,645
That will be my second gift.

958
00:56:29,429 --> 00:56:30,778
To see what is
right in front of you.

959
00:56:31,997 --> 00:56:33,607
What is right in front of me?

960
00:56:33,694 --> 00:56:35,566
What? What does that mean?

961
00:56:40,179 --> 00:56:42,355
My ship.

962
00:56:42,442 --> 00:56:46,403
You ask for a weapon,
and yet you sail one.

963
00:56:46,490 --> 00:56:47,926
Yes.

964
00:56:48,013 --> 00:56:50,711
Why didn’t I see that before?

965
00:56:50,798 --> 00:56:51,886
The ship is a weapon.

966
00:56:55,629 --> 00:56:58,415
Sometimes you are so
worried about what is ahead,

967
00:56:58,502 --> 00:57:01,983
that you fail to see what
is directly in front of you.

968
00:57:02,070 --> 00:57:03,768
Take heed, Odysseus.

969
00:57:03,855 --> 00:57:06,553
You will defeat the
monsters that surround you.

970
00:57:17,216 --> 00:57:18,609
Odysseus?

971
00:57:18,696 --> 00:57:20,611
Odysseus, you’re steering us...

972
00:57:20,698 --> 00:57:22,482
You steer us right towards it!

973
00:57:22,569 --> 00:57:24,092
Right towards the
jaws of the beast!

974
00:57:24,179 --> 00:57:26,617
Not the jaws!
It’s belly!

975
00:57:28,662 --> 00:57:30,534
We’ll run it through
with the prow!

976
00:57:30,621 --> 00:57:32,057
The ship will be our sword!

977
00:57:32,144 --> 00:57:34,276
Yes! Yes!

978
00:57:43,372 --> 00:57:45,418
Odysseus, it’s
not going to work!

979
00:57:45,505 --> 00:57:48,508
We’ve got to turn her
around or we’re done for!

980
00:57:48,595 --> 00:57:50,379
The current is too strong!

981
00:57:50,467 --> 00:57:51,598
It’s not working!

982
00:57:54,645 --> 00:57:57,125
She’s going to eat us alive!

983
00:57:57,212 --> 00:57:58,823
Hold fast, Polites!

984
00:57:58,910 --> 00:58:01,216
We need momentum!

985
00:58:01,303 --> 00:58:02,217
I’m trying!

986
00:58:18,407 --> 00:58:20,801
That’s the only
belly-full you’ll get today!

987
00:58:20,888 --> 00:58:22,063
Yes, Odysseus!

988
00:58:27,547 --> 00:58:28,548
Yes!

989
00:58:34,554 --> 00:58:36,077
Come on, Telemachus.

990
00:58:36,164 --> 00:58:38,906
You must be ready to fight!

991
00:58:40,778 --> 00:58:41,648
Or we’ll lose everything.

992
00:58:44,521 --> 00:58:46,871
Come home, father.
Quickly.

993
00:58:57,969 --> 00:59:00,972
The cloak you make
for me is nearly finished.

994
00:59:01,059 --> 00:59:04,715
And there are unwanted
guests in your house.

995
00:59:04,802 --> 00:59:06,586
About the cloak...

996
00:59:06,673 --> 00:59:08,066
Alas, my son.

997
00:59:08,153 --> 00:59:11,417
I fear there is still
so much work to be done.

998
00:59:11,504 --> 00:59:14,551
This cloak may take
many days to finish.

999
00:59:16,378 --> 00:59:18,163
You’re undoing what you’ve done.

1000
00:59:18,250 --> 00:59:19,381
If only I could.

1001
00:59:21,383 --> 00:59:23,385
Mother, you’ve
done nothing wrong.

1002
00:59:23,472 --> 00:59:24,865
It’s just...

1003
00:59:24,952 --> 00:59:26,780
What?

1004
00:59:26,867 --> 00:59:28,695
Undoing the cloak every night.

1005
00:59:31,306 --> 00:59:32,743
Sometimes clever
is better than good.

1006
00:59:36,311 --> 00:59:39,010
I just pray it buys
us the time he needs.

1007
00:59:39,097 --> 00:59:41,142
And if it doesn’t, then
I will be ready to fight.

1008
00:59:42,883 --> 00:59:45,190
No one is taking
this kingdom from us.

1009
00:59:46,844 --> 00:59:47,714
No one.

1010
00:59:49,455 --> 00:59:50,761
And I will be ready to fight.

1011
01:00:08,866 --> 01:00:09,736
Odysseus?

1012
01:00:11,782 --> 01:00:13,740
Odysseus.

1013
01:00:13,827 --> 01:00:17,309
Treacherous waters ahead.

1014
01:00:17,396 --> 01:00:19,833
♪ Come to us

1015
01:00:19,920 --> 01:00:21,400
That’s treacherous
waters indeed.

1016
01:00:21,487 --> 01:00:23,054
It means we’re getting
close to land now.

1017
01:00:23,141 --> 01:00:24,882
Huh.

1018
01:00:24,969 --> 01:00:27,667
That’s the first good
thing I’ve heard all day.

1019
01:00:27,754 --> 01:00:29,451
We must stay clear
of those rocks.

1020
01:00:29,538 --> 01:00:31,671
And I have less
than a day before...

1021
01:00:31,758 --> 01:00:34,195
Yeah, before someone claims
what is rightfully yours.

1022
01:00:34,282 --> 01:00:36,894
I know, my king.
I know.

1023
01:00:36,981 --> 01:00:37,982
I am no king here.

1024
01:00:39,810 --> 01:00:41,376
There is only one
king in the ocean.

1025
01:00:41,463 --> 01:00:43,291
And I hold no favor
with him now.

1026
01:00:43,378 --> 01:00:44,815
♪ Come to us

1027
01:00:44,902 --> 01:00:46,120
Odysseus...

1028
01:00:46,207 --> 01:00:48,296
Can you hear that?

1029
01:00:48,383 --> 01:00:50,385
- Music?
- No.

1030
01:00:50,472 --> 01:00:52,083
No, not the songs...

1031
01:00:52,170 --> 01:00:53,606
of the sirens, no.

1032
01:00:53,693 --> 01:00:55,564
♪ Come to us

1033
01:00:58,655 --> 01:00:59,612
No.

1034
01:00:59,699 --> 01:01:01,570
Polites, no. No!

1035
01:01:01,658 --> 01:01:02,876
Protect yourself!

1036
01:01:02,963 --> 01:01:04,051
No!

1037
01:01:04,138 --> 01:01:05,749
- Sirens.
- Not the sirens!

1038
01:01:05,836 --> 01:01:06,967
They call to me.

1039
01:01:07,054 --> 01:01:09,143
No!

1040
01:01:09,230 --> 01:01:10,884
Cover your ears!

1041
01:01:10,971 --> 01:01:12,146
Protect yourself, Polites!

1042
01:01:16,847 --> 01:01:18,370
They speak to me.

1043
01:01:18,457 --> 01:01:20,502
Cover your ears!
Protect yourself!

1044
01:01:20,589 --> 01:01:21,547
They want me to get my reward.

1045
01:01:23,810 --> 01:01:25,029
Rewards for a good man.

1046
01:01:25,116 --> 01:01:27,988
No. No!

1047
01:01:28,075 --> 01:01:29,163
They speak to me.

1048
01:01:32,036 --> 01:01:35,430
I hear the song.
It’s in my head!

1049
01:01:35,517 --> 01:01:36,431
They speak to me.

1050
01:01:41,872 --> 01:01:45,658
Telemachus is the
real problem here.

1051
01:01:45,745 --> 01:01:49,575
The woman?
I can handle.

1052
01:01:49,662 --> 01:01:50,924
But the boy?

1053
01:01:53,231 --> 01:01:58,366
Any loyal dog would
struggle obeying a new master.
